Raw dev notes -- GPU, XLSX, and the never-ending quest for robust AI agents

**1. Project Background**
This project, Weaverai_Agents_MCP, is essentially the brain and nervous system for our AI agents. It's all about building a robust, scalable platform where these agents can perform various tasks, from understanding spoken language to crunching data in spreadsheets, all while efficiently managing precious GPU resources. The goal is to make these agents smart, reliable, and secure.

**2. What was done in this period**
Man, this past couple of weeks felt like a whirlwind. We kicked things off by really digging into **GPU resource management**. I built out pre-flight checks for deployments, making sure we had enough VRAM and persistent storage before spinning up new services. There was a lot of planning around how to share a single, beefy 97GB GPU across multiple models, and even considering downsizing our default chat model for efficiency.

A huge win was getting **GPU-accelerated speaker diarization** working. This was a beast. We moved it from CPU to a dedicated service running on GPU, specifically tackling some quirks with Blackwell architecture (sm_120). It involved supporting new audio formats like m4a/AAC and wiring it all up to the transcription service. On the core platform side, I added a Kubernetes health board for a quick glance at service status, and implemented durable result recovery with 3-day retention, plus some secure-by-default endpoints.

Then came the **XLSX processing sprint**. This was a massive undertaking. We designed and implemented a whole pipeline for handling spreadsheets, including features like cloning styles, formatting, and scanning sequences. A big part of this was integrating LLMs to help "find data" within sheets, which required building a robust sandbox runner with AST import gates, isolated subprocesses, and Linux rlimits to prevent any nasty code leaks. We also added support for right-to-left languages like Hebrew, and fixed issues with large file uploads in the chat UI, showing token counts and better error messages. The design for this whole builder service went through a rigorous Q&A and got approved, which was a relief.

Finally, I integrated a "DeepWiki" helper for knowledge retrieval, fixed some critical disk-full/OOM issues with our Langfuse observability stack (ClickHouse was *not* happy), and did a crucial security audit to redact secrets from our n8n workflow backups.

**3. What was hard or surprising**
The **GPU diarization on Blackwell** was a real head-scratcher. Getting the right Torch/CUDA versions to play nice with the new hardware felt like wrestling an octopus. The initial fix was just a path change, but then realizing we needed a *separate* pod for it was a whole architectural shift.

The **XLSX sandbox** was another beast. Ensuring true isolation for arbitrary code execution, even LLM-generated, is terrifying. Dealing with fence-leaks and making sure the LLM output was actually usable and didn't break things took a lot of iteration and live-model verification. And the sheer number of edge cases for XLSX files – different formats, VBA macros, conversion fidelity – meant a ton of planning and design docs. We even had to plan for adversarial escape tests.

Also, the **Langfuse ClickHouse issue** was sneaky. It wasn't an `EPERM` error like it seemed, but just plain old disk-full and OOM. Classic "check the logs more carefully" moment, but it cost us some valuable debugging time.

**4. Personal reflection**
This period felt like a huge leap in complexity and responsibility. I'm proud of how we tackled the GPU resource challenges and got the diarization working reliably. It felt good to really dig into the hardware and low-level optimization. The XLSX feature, though, was a monster. It pushed me to think about security, sandboxing, and LLM integration in ways I hadn't before. It was a constant battle between shipping features and ensuring bulletproof reliability and security. There were moments of frustration, especially with the sandbox, where I felt like I was chasing my tail, but seeing it finally work and pass tests was incredibly satisfying. It reinforced the importance of meticulous planning and robust testing, especially when dealing with user-uploaded content and LLM-generated code. It also highlighted how much I still have to learn about building truly resilient and secure systems.

**5. What's next**
Time to refine the XLSX builder and integrate it fully into the agent workflows.
